[center][size=6][b][color=#b48cff]S3MAPHORE[/color][/b][/size]
[size=4][i]Your music. Your rules.[/i][/size]

[b][i]Brought to You by DreamWeave-MP[/i][/b][/center]

[center][size=4][b]Modern, contextual music management for OpenMW.[/b][/size]

No compromises. No bullshit. Just a powerful playlist system that lets your music respond to the world around you.[/center]

[heading][center][color=#b48cff]REQUIREMENTS[/color][/center][/heading]

[list]
[*][b]OpenMW 0.51 or newer[/b] — [url=https://openmw.org/downloads]Download OpenMW[/url][/*]
[*][b]H3lp Yours3lf[/b] — [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/morrowind/mods/56417]Download H3lp Yours3lf[/url][/*]
[*][b]S3maphore[/b] — this mod[/*]
[/list]

[heading][center][color=#b48cff]INSTALLATION — START HERE[/color][/center][/heading]

[center][size=5][b][color=#ffcc66]You must enable 00 Core and S3maphore.esp.[/color][/b][/size]
[b]Everything else is optional.[/b][/center]

[size=4][b]Using an OpenMW mod manager[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]1.[/b] Install [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/morrowind/mods/56417]H3lp Yours3lf[/url] first.[/*]
[*][b]2.[/b] Install S3maphore with your OpenMW mod manager.[/*]
[*][b]3.[/b] Keep the [font=Courier New]00 Core[/font] data directory enabled.[/*]
[*][b]4.[/b] Enable [font=Courier New]S3maphore.esp[/font] in your OpenMW content list.[/*]
[*][b]5.[/b] Enable any optional numbered modules you want. Start with [font=Courier New]01 Tamriel Rebuilt Playlists[/font], [font=Courier New]02 Project Cyrodiil Playlists[/font], or [font=Courier New]03 Muse Expansion Playlists[/font] only if you use the corresponding music.[/*]
[*][b]6.[/b] Launch the game. Vanilla Explore and Battle music work immediately.[/*]
[/list]

[color=#ffcc66][b]Do not enable every module blindly.[/b][/color] Most optional modules are playlist definitions and need matching tracks from another mod. If no matching tracks exist, that playlist simply stays inactive.

[size=4][b]Manual OpenMW installation[/b][/size]

S3maphore is packaged as several OpenMW data directories. Do [b]not[/b] flatten the archive into Morrowind's [font=Courier New]Data Files[/font] directory. Keep the folders separate and add the ones you want to [font=Courier New]openmw.cfg[/font].

For example:

[code]data="/path/to/S3maphore/00 Core"
data="/path/to/S3maphore/03 Muse Expansion Playlists"[/code]

Add [font=Courier New]S3maphore.esp[/font] from [font=Courier New]00 Core[/font] to your OpenMW content list. Add other numbered data directories only when you want their playlists.

[color=#d4d4d8][b]Important:[/b] the module numbers are labels, not load-order numbers. They do not need to be enabled in numerical order.[/color]

[heading][center][color=#b48cff]WHAT S3MAPHORE DOES[/color][/center][/heading]

OpenMW's music system was dehardcoded during the 0.49 development cycle. The underlying mechanism is extremely powerful, but the public playlist API was removed before release. S3maphore restores that missing layer as a full replacement for OpenMW's built-in music handling.

S3maphore works through playlists, like MUSE and Dynamic Music, but playlists are open-ended. A playlist creator can define any conditions under which music should play: location, region, cell, faction, enemy, weather, time, tileset, magic effect, or any combination of them.

The system has three moving parts:

[list]
[*][b]Playlist[/b] — a contextual collection of tracks.[/*]
[*][b]Priority[/b] — decides which valid playlist wins when several conditions apply.[/*]
[*][b]Playdeck[/b] — the complete set of playlists discovered in your OpenMW installation.[/*]
[/list]

When the game state changes, S3maphore deals the highest-priority valid playlist from the playdeck. Battle music is not magic; it is simply a playlist with a combat rule and a high priority.

[heading][center][color=#b48cff]MODULES[/color][/center][/heading]

S3maphore is divided into modules. Every module is an OpenMW data directory containing playlist definitions, music, or both.

[size=4][b]Required[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][font=Courier New]00 Core[/font] — required. Provides the S3maphore engine and the vanilla [font=Courier New]Music/Explore[/font] and [font=Courier New]Music/Battle[/font] playlists. It does not include replacement music.[/*]
[/list]

